WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.709
OpenAI turns text into vectors of about fifteen hundred numbers, and stores billions of them.

00:00:06.709 --> 00:00:11.418
To find the most similar, the exact way compares your query against every single one.

00:00:11.418 --> 00:00:16.127
At a million vectors that is over a billion multiply-adds, six gigabytes scanned, for one query.

00:00:16.127 --> 00:00:20.836
OpenAI runs billions of queries.

00:00:20.836 --> 00:00:25.545
The exact way does not stand a chance.

00:00:25.545 --> 00:00:29.423
The exact index is simple.

00:00:29.423 --> 00:00:33.300
Store every vector, and for each query, measure the distance to all of them, then take the closest.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:37.178
The answer is exact, but the cost is order N every time.

00:00:37.178 --> 00:00:41.056
Double the data, double the work.

00:00:41.056 --> 00:00:44.933
At a billion vectors, one lookup reads the whole dataset.

00:00:44.933 --> 00:00:48.811
The only way to scale is to stop looking at most of it.

00:00:48.811 --> 00:00:52.924
So how does HNSW find the answer without scanning?

00:00:52.924 --> 00:00:57.038
It is a graph, built in layers, once, as the vectors arrive.

00:00:57.038 --> 00:01:01.151
At the bottom, every vector links to its nearest neighbours, short local hops.

00:01:01.151 --> 00:01:05.264
Then a few vectors are promoted up a layer, with longer links.

00:01:05.264 --> 00:01:09.377
Fewer still go up again, with the longest links of all, the express lanes.

00:01:09.377 --> 00:01:13.491
That hierarchy is the whole index.

00:01:13.491 --> 00:01:17.604
Short roads at the bottom, highways on top.

00:01:17.604 --> 00:01:22.208
Now, how does a search know which way to go?

00:01:22.208 --> 00:01:26.812
It holds the query, so at any node it can measure how far each neighbour sits from it.

00:01:26.812 --> 00:01:31.416
It enters at the one node on the top layer.

00:01:31.416 --> 00:01:36.021
It steps to the neighbour closest to the query, a long jump on the express lane.

00:01:36.021 --> 00:01:40.625
When nothing is closer, it drops a layer and hops again, refining.

00:01:40.625 --> 00:01:45.229
At the bottom layer it lands on the nearest.

00:01:45.229 --> 00:01:49.833
The highways get it close, the local roads finish the job, a few hops instead of a scan.

00:01:49.833 --> 00:01:55.557
Now the trade-off, side by side.

00:01:55.557 --> 00:02:01.281
The exact scan compares the query to every vector, a billion multiply-adds, six gigabytes, every query.

00:02:01.281 --> 00:02:07.004
The fix is to build the index once, and then a query touches only a tiny fraction of the data, the few near it, in well under a millisecond.

00:02:07.004 --> 00:02:12.728
Same answer almost always, a hundred to a thousand times less work.

00:02:12.728 --> 00:02:18.475
A vector database is approximate nearest neighbor search over embeddings.

00:02:18.475 --> 00:02:24.222
It trades the occasional missed match for sub- millisecond lookups across billions of vectors, which an exact scan could never serve.

00:02:24.222 --> 00:02:29.969
HNSW, the layered graph, is the production default.

00:02:29.969 --> 00:02:35.716
Fitting all those vectors in memory is the other half, quantization, next.
